social media has played a big role  during this pandemic and as we all know  it can sometimes be misleading 8 news  now reporter victoria saha spoke to a  social media expert today to give us  some tips on how we can know what's real  and what's not victoria  it's 2021 and most of us now have one of  these right whether it's to make calls  or text or scroll through social media  to get our latest scoop from around the  world but information that you see might  not always be true we are now more than  a year and half into the pandemic and  social media is filled with both facts  and rumors rumors such as the virus as a  hoax to there's a microchip in the  vaccine to most recently people thinking  that a horse dewormer will cure you of  cobit we recently reported people in  nevada have been poisoned by it and it  all starts with a simple rumor going  viral making many believe it  communications professor emma francis  bloomfield of unlv says this is where it  becomes dangerous  this idea that horse dewormer reduces  inflammation right if that's true and  why people use it on their horses people  then extrapolate that into oh it will be  useful for humans as well that's where  the problem is right there's like a sort  of a kernel or a nugget of truth but the  way people are applying it and spinning  it off is what makes it really dangerous  she says the big problem is that social  media platforms are used by both  credible and non-credible sources which  can be confusing catchy headlines can  grab your attention quick but you should  always verify it  now social media hasn't been all that  negative during the pandemic when most  of you got vaccinated you posted on  social media that you got vaccinated or  you posted your vaccine card professor  bloomfield says this is a way to spread  positive messages reporting live from  the newsroom victoria saha 8 news now
 

